11.4  The page cache

TL;DR
  - The page cache keeps file data in RAM after it has been read or written, so
    the next access doesn't touch the disk.
  - It uses spare memory and shrinks by itself when apps need more: there's
    nothing to free by hand.
  - Written data first lands in the cache ("dirty") and reaches the disk within
    about 30 seconds; sync writes it now.

      free -h
      grep -E 'Dirty|Writeback' /proc/meminfo
      sync

On your machine
  RAM       10.5 GiB available of 15.5 GiB (68%) · 1.1 GiB completely free
  Cache     7.5 GiB in buff/cache, 604.0 MiB of it shared (tmpfs)
  Swap      256.0 MiB used of 8.0 GiB · swappiness 60

Pitfalls
  - Emptying the cache (echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches, as root) is for
    benchmarks only: the machine is slower afterwards, until it refills.
  - Pulling out a USB drive right after a copy can lose data still in the cache:
    eject it, or run sync, first.
  - A big copy can fill RAM with dirty data, then make every program that writes
    wait while it drains.
  - Timing a disk twice in a row times the RAM the second time.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does Linux decide what to drop?

    It tells pages used once from pages used again and again, and drops the
    first kind first. That way one big copy doesn't push out the files you use
    all the time. Since kernel 6.1, many distros use the multi-generational LRU,
    a finer version of the same idea.

  ▾ When does written data reach the disk?

    Background writing starts when dirty data passes vm.dirty_background_ratio
    (10% of available memory by default), or when it is about 30 seconds old.
    Past vm.dirty_ratio (20%), programs that write are made to wait until enough
    is on disk.

Related
  Commands  free, sync
  Paths     /proc/meminfo, /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches
  Hub       idk memory Memory
  See also  idk 11.1 free vs available
  See also  idk 11.3 Swap and swappiness
